  "generated_text": "To determine the least possible number of students who have both brown eyes and a lunch box, we need to consider the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\n1. Let \\( B \\) be the set of students with brown eyes.\n2. Let \\( L \\) be the set of students with a lunch box.\n3. We know \\( |B| = 12 \\) and \\( |L| = 20 \\).\n4. The total number of students in the class is 30.\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |B \\cup L| = |B| + |L| - |B \\cap L| \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 30 = 12 + 20 - |B \\cap L| \\]\n\nSolving for \\( |B \\cap L| \\):\n\\[ 30 = 32 - |B \\cap L| \\]\n\\[ |B \\cap L| = 32 - 30 = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the least possible number of students who have both brown eyes and a lunch box is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[2, '2']",
    "gold_answer": "Since $20$ students have a lunch box, $10$ students do not have a lunch box.  So at most $10$ students with brown eyes do not have a lunch box, and at least $\\boxed{2}$ students with brown eyes must have a lunch box.",
    "is_correct": true
